<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Marine Corps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as US Marine Corps</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Marine Corps is the maritime land force of the United States, founded in 1775 and serving under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Navy. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries Marines landed repeatedly in the Pacific and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, supporting the overthrow of the Hawaiian monarchy in 1893 and occupying Nicaragua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. They later fought major amphibious campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps.jpg" alt="United States Marine Corps" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_United_States_Marine_Corps.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…wars between party chiefs continued into the twentieth century, often with foreign backing: in 1911 the former president Manuel Bonilla invaded with mercenaries financed by Samuel Zemurray and regained power, and <b>United States Marines</b> landed several times between 1903 and 1925, notably during the civil war of 1924.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>United States Marines</b> landed in 1912 to support a conservative government against a liberal rising, in which the nationalist general Benjamín Zeledón was killed, and a legation guard remained until 1925.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second World War up to four hundred Navajo code talkers carried messages for the <b>US Marine Corps</b> in a code built on their own language, in which the word for “turtle” stood for a tank; the Japanese never deciphered it.</blockquote><a href="/culture/navajo">Navajo · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>An Act of Congress created the <b>US Marine Corps</b> as a permanent service, reviving the Continental Marines of the Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-11">11 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The date is celebrated as the birthday of the <b>United States Marine Corps</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-10">10 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After years of civil war and default on foreign debt, President Woodrow Wilson sent <b>Marines</b> to Santo Domingo in May 1916.</blockquote><a href="/state/military-government-of-santo-domingo">Military Government of Santo Domingo</a></li></ul></section>
